    "problem_statement": "Incorrect behavior in StepTickTimeFactory with auto_tick_seconds\n\nDescription\n\nThe `StepTickTimeFactory` class has incorrect behavior when using auto_tick_seconds. The time doesn't increment properly when using both auto tick and manual tick operations.\n\nHere's a simple example to reproduce the issue:\n\n```python\nimport datetime\nfrom freezegun import freeze_time\n\nfirst_time = datetime.datetime(2020, 1, 14, 0, 0, 0)\nwith freeze_time(first_time, auto_tick_seconds=2) as frozen_time:\n    # Check initial time\n    print(f\"Initial time: {datetime.datetime.now()}\")\n    \n    # Manual tick\n    frozen_time.tick(1.5)\n    print(f\"After manual tick: {datetime.datetime.now()}\")\n    \n    # Wait for auto tick to happen\n    print(f\"After auto tick: {datetime.datetime.now()}\")\n    \n    # Another manual tick\n    frozen_time.tick(1.5)\n    print(f\"After second manual tick: {datetime.datetime.now()}\")\n```\n\nExpected output:\n```\nInitial time: 2020-01-14 00:00:00\nAfter manual tick: 2020-01-14 00:00:01.500000\nAfter auto tick: 2020-01-14 00:00:03.500000\nAfter second manual tick: 2020-01-14 00:00:05\n```\n\nActual output:\n```\nInitial time: 2020-01-14 00:00:00\nAfter manual tick: 2020-01-14 00:00:01\nAfter auto tick: 2020-01-14 00:00:01\nAfter second manual tick: 2020-01-14 00:00:01\n```\n\nThe time doesn't increment correctly when combining auto tick with manual tick operations. This makes it impossible to properly test time-dependent code that requires both automatic time progression and manual time adjustments."
  },
